config:
schema_version: 1
identity:
  model: gru
  version: v028
  parent: gru_v023
  status: finished
description: 'v023''s exact recipe and architecture with ONE change: distill_weight
  0.5 -> 1.0. Same hidden 128 (225,922 params), same teacher (dozken''s reset2k replayed
  as exact labels), same loss otherwise, same optimiser, same batch 16, 3 epochs,
  chunk 1,000, state reset every 2,000. This is the distillation-weight experiment
  and nothing else.'
hypothesis: 'Our students land BELOW their teacher, which is the signature of too
  LITTLE teacher signal rather than too much. The evidence is quantitative and matched
  on compression: dozken''s distil160 is 72% of his teacher and GAINED +0.0009 over
  it, while our v022 at 74% LOST -0.0049 against the same teacher. That is a ~0.006
  shortfall in our distillation at equal compression. Meanwhile the student sits between
  the two things it is trained on: hard-labels-only (v021) scores 0.678575, the teacher
  scores 0.690168, and v023 scores 0.684681 -- roughly midway, so doubling the teacher
  term should pull it toward the teacher without anything else changing. If it does,
  the knob is real and 2.0 is the next value; if it does not, the shortfall is not
  the WEIGHT of the teacher signal but its CONTENT, which points at feature distillation
  instead.'
change_vs_parent: distill_weight 0.5 -> 1.0 ONLY. The term is dw * weighted_MSE(teacher_clipped,
  pred_clipped), so this doubles the teacher term against an unchanged weighted-Pearson
  hard-label term -- the teacher goes from ~1/3 to ~1/2 of the total gradient.
